---
import Layout from '../layouts.astro';
const base = `${import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '')}/`;
---
<Layout title="Individual & team workflows · Style Constitution">
  <div class="page-head"><p class="eyebrow">From finding to decision</p><h1>A useful next step.<br />Evidence you can revisit.</h1><p class="lead">For people building websites with coding agents, and teams maintaining shared interface rules.</p><p class="micro">These Studio upgrades are in the current development build. The published v0.8.0 archive does not include them yet. <a href="https://github.com/sulabhdubey/astra-fable-styleguide-mcp/blob/main/docs/INSTALLATION.md">Build from the reviewed source branch ↗</a></p></div>
  <section class="section" id="individual" aria-labelledby="individual-title">
    <div class="section-intro"><div><p class="eyebrow">Individual builders</p><h2 id="individual-title">Give your agent<br />a concrete finding.</h2></div><p class="muted">You need a trusted local static HTML or React/Vite project and Node. Studio guides supported setup. It cannot check any public website just from its URL.</p></div>
    <div class="steps"><article><span class="step-number">01</span><h3>Check your project</h3><p>Choose the configured dialog and measurement targets. See the actual result, expected rule, viewport and anything the check could not verify.</p></article><article><span class="step-number">02</span><h3>Prepare a focused brief</h3><p>Select findings and review the brief before copying. It carries source and rule identifiers, relative file labels and recheck steps. No AI service is contacted.</p></article><article><span class="step-number">03</span><h3>Review and recheck</h3><p>Use your existing coding agent to propose changes. Review those changes, rebuild Vite when needed, and run the checks again. The brief itself grants no write authority.</p></article></div>
    <div class="card"><h3>Try the complete local example</h3><pre><code>stylecon demo /absolute/path/to/new-demo
stylecon studio /absolute/path/to/new-demo --evidence /absolute/path/to/private-evidence</code></pre><p>Create the private evidence directory first, outside the demo workspace. Select <code>project</code>, run checks, and inspect the failing button. Select its finding, then choose <strong>Prepare selected findings</strong>.</p><p class="muted">The supplied correction also lets you try preview, apply, recheck and undo without an AI account. Your coding agent may have its own costs.</p></div>
  </section>
  <section class="section" id="teams" aria-labelledby="teams-title">
    <div class="section-intro"><div><p class="eyebrow">Teams & enterprise evaluation</p><h2 id="teams-title">Agree on the rules.<br />Keep the evidence scoped.</h2></div><p class="muted">Start with one supported component and an advisory check. Keep policy approval and production decisions in your existing repository workflow.</p></div>
    <div class="steps"><article><span class="step-number">01</span><h3>Review team rules</h3><p>Create and export a constitution. In Studio, create a snapshot, review the old and proposed rules, then adopt its exact hash for the selected project.</p></article><article><span class="step-number">02</span><h3>Revisit the evidence</h3><p>Private history survives Studio restarts. Compare runs with the same configuration, rules and recorded coverage. Different rules cannot erase an earlier failure.</p></article><article><span class="step-number">03</span><h3>Review a pull request</h3><p>The existing CI workflow binds evidence to Git source and shows new, existing, resolved and incomplete findings. Reviewers decide how it affects the PR.</p></article></div>
    <div class="split"><article class="card"><h3>What stays local</h3><p>Studio listens on loopback. Reports and history stay in the private directory you choose. No project account, telemetry or automatic cloud upload is required.</p><p>Briefs can contain private project text. Review them before sharing. History checksums detect accidental changes; they are not signed attestations.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>What an evaluation must establish</h3><p>Confirm support for your component, compare results with your current tests, and record unresolved coverage. Check whether the workflow saves your team effort on real changes.</p><p>This local tool does not supply hosted workspaces, organization identity, SSO, role management, compliance certification or a service-level agreement.</p></article></div>
  </section>
  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="review-title"><div class="section-intro"><div><p class="eyebrow">For clients & reviewers</p><h2 id="review-title">Open the results.<br />No tools to install.</h2></div><p class="muted">The developer prepares an offline review packet in Studio. You receive a readable HTML document with every recorded outcome and visible evidence gaps.</p></div><div class="card"><h3>Less project detail to disclose</h3><p>Numbered elements replace private target names. Measurements, viewports and evidence identifiers stay visible; source paths and free-form diagnostics are omitted. Ask the sender which page and elements the numbers describe, and request context for missing values.</p><p>The sender reviews the complete packet before downloading. It opens without scripts or network access. It is a historical observation, not a live check, signed approval or full accessibility audit.</p><p><a href="https://github.com/sulabhdubey/astra-fable-styleguide-mcp/blob/main/docs/REVIEW_PACKETS.md">Review packet workflow ↗</a> · Included in the development source; pending publication.</p></div></section>
  <section class="section"><h2>Start small. Inspect what changed.</h2><div class="actions"><a class="button primary" href={`${base}demo/`}>Try the browser sample →</a><a class="button" href={`${base}start/`}>Installation & agent setup →</a><a href="https://github.com/sulabhdubey/astra-fable-styleguide-mcp/blob/main/docs/PR_REGRESSIONS.md">Existing CI workflow ↗</a></div></section>
</Layout>
